this morning a Washington Post  investigation focuses on Russian  government interference in our election  in August the CIA gave then President  Obama a report quote drawn from sourcing  deep inside the Russian government it  detailed Russian President Vladimir  Putin's direct involvement in a cyber  campaign to disrupt and discredit the  u.s. presidential race national security  correspondent Greg Miller co-wrote The  Washington Post article good morning  good morning Greg first of all to your  team it's incredible read and great  reporting I know you spoke with dozens  of officials why do you call this the  crime of the century and what was in  that report that was given to President  Obama I mean I we called in political  terms the crime of the century because I  think it was an unprecedented assault on  a very fundamental mechanism of American  democracy the way we elect our president  and it was largely to a large degree  successful I mean I'm not saying that it  determined the outcome but we're  certainly still living with the  instability and the uncertainty that  this helped create about our electoral  process and we thought it was really  important to spend a good amount of  reporting time and energy trying to  understand the magnitude of this and why  the Obama administration handled it the  way that it did  Greggy if it was that monumental why did  not the administration at the time do  more in response well that's that  question is at the heart of this story  and that's one of the questions that we  set out to try to answer and there as  you might expect there are several  answers there are some sort of basic  tactical answers the White House was  very concerned that if it acted before  the election Putin might just escalate  it might just provoke even more cyber  activity by Russia things could get a  lot worse was there worried and then  there was a political layer to their  concerns they really were consumed with  worry that if they acted if the Obama  administration acted they would be  accused of using intelligence of using  national security as an issue to try to  help Hillary Clinton and they tried to  avoid that and as we say in the story  you know in a weird way that ends up  boomeranging on them that ends up  meaning that politics did play a major  role in  was a serious factor in their decisions  on how and whether to respond to Russia  and and Greg you're reporting show that  this went directly to President Putin in  the past he had said well if it was a  Russian person that he was a patriot he  is repeatedly denied that he was  directly involved in this your  investigation has shown otherwise right  right I mean that's one of the main  revelations in our story and it's at the  very top of the story the CIA had  blockbuster intelligence at the very  beginning of August last year of  catching Putin's own instructions on  this operation and its objective helped  elect Trump Greg Udall so detail regret  by a number of Obama administration  officials about perhaps their failure to  respond what did you learn I think that  that was fairly widespread among many of  the Obama administration officials we  talked to not all of them of course  those close to the president really  protect his legacy and really defend  what they did in the decisions that they  made but we spoke with many people who  look back on this period with a sense of  remorse and regret and sort of can't  understand themselves why why that  administration couldn't muster couldn't  find a way to do more yeah I mean you  detail in fact even the Secretary of  State John Kerry giving an action memo  that included a package of retaliatory  measures and it was the response from  the White House yet to me that's one of  the more interesting sort of moments in  this in this story that we're telling  Kerry before the election the White  House has decided it's not going to do  anything punitive toward Russia before  the election but Kerry comes right at  the last minute says fine  let's do something right afterward then  we have to we have to respond some way  and we should do it right after the  votes are counted he's basically told no  we're not listening to that right now I  mean it just spoked speaks to the deep  reluctance to take this on but Greg can  you explain why this is so important  because I saw an interview the other day  with a voter who said listen I'm sick of  hearing about this Russian investigation  there's nothing there it didn't change  though they do not tamper with the votes  could you really break down briefly why  this matters I think it matters for many  many reasons  one of them is that US intelligence  agencies have already warned this is  going to happen  and again the US hasn't really figured  out how to safeguard its electoral  process from further interference by  Russia and I mean whether this affected  the outcome whether we have president  Trump as a result of this operation we  may never get an answer to that question  but the instability that we're living  with in Washington right now and the  doubt  that many Americans have about the  security surrounding our basic sort of  fundamental mechanisms of democracy was  an primary Russian objective and one  that they must feel that they  accomplished all right Greg Miller thank  you very much we also echo Nora's  compliment to you great reporting thank  you so much so much thank you
 

